The optional Linux connection diagnostic uses ws 8.22.0 (MIT), installed as a
pinned npm dependency. Its license is distributed with that dependency.

test/fixtures/codex-security-draft-input-schema.json contains the draft tool input
schema published in @openai/codex-security 0.5.1 (Apache-2.0).

test/fixtures/trivy-offline.json contains output from Trivy 0.76.1 (Apache-2.0),
including Trivy rule text and reference URLs.
Source: https://github.com/aquasecurity/trivy/tree/v0.76.1
License: https://github.com/aquasecurity/trivy/blob/v0.76.1/LICENSE

src/adapters.ts records the global path allowlist regular expressions from
Gitleaks 8.30.1 (MIT) as coverage exclusions.
Source: https://github.com/gitleaks/gitleaks/tree/v8.30.1
License: https://github.com/gitleaks/gitleaks/blob/v8.30.1/LICENSE

src/advisory-catalog.ts records advisory identifiers and affected and fixed
versions from public security advisories by PHP, Node.js, Next.js, NGINX,
GitLab and Apache Tomcat. Each record links its source; no advisory text is
copied.

The evaluation pairs under eval/pairs/ vendor the minimal files of released
dependencies, at the exact versions, for offline regression tests:
- lodash 4.17.20 and 4.17.21 (MIT) in eval/pairs/js/, with its LICENSE.
- PyYAML 5.3.1 and 5.4 (MIT) in eval/pairs/python/, with its LICENSE.
golang.org/x/text v0.3.6 and v0.3.7 (BSD-3-Clause) are named by version in
eval/pairs/go/*/go.mod only; their source is not vendored.
